NAME
    dir - list a directory, sorted

SYNOPSIS
    dir [-R] [-S] [path | glob | file]

DESCRIPTION
    Lists a directory: the current one with no argument, or the one a
    path names. A glob (* or ? in the last component, case folded)
    lists the matching entries of that directory, files and
    directories alike; a file lists just that file's line. "dir: not
    found" when the name does not exist. dir is /bin/dir.

    A line is the size right-justified in 7 columns (blank for a
    directory), two spaces, the name ("/" after a directory), and
    "  @AAAA" when the file has a load address (a program, a file
    saved from memory; the host tool gives its files $B000):

             98  fruit.txt  @B000
                 MAN/

    "." and ".." are left out. Entries are sorted by name in byte
    order, so A-Z come before a-z. -S sorts by size, largest first,
    ties by name; a directory counts as size 0.

    -R lists the directory, then every directory below it, depth
    first, as a block of its own headed by a blank line and "/PATH:"
    (as ls -R does); a glob filters every level.

    The shell also has a built-in dir, used only when /bin/dir is
    missing: unsorted, with "." and "..", <DIR> for a directory, and
    the count at the end.

OPTIONS
    -R    also list every directory below, a block each (or -r)
    -S    sort by size, largest first (or -s)

LIMITS
    255 entries per directory are sorted and shown (a new directory
    holds 62, /src's 254); past that dir says "dir: over 255 entries
    in a directory, the rest not shown". -R descends 8 levels ("dir: deeper than 8
    levels, not all shown"). A glob pattern is at most 15 characters.
    Sizes are 32-bit (see num).

EXAMPLES
    dir                  the current directory
    dir /bin             by path
    dir -S /bin          the largest programs first
    dir /*.txt           the .TXT files of the root
    dir /fruit.txt       one file's line
    dir -R /             the whole disk, a block per directory

NOTES
    Changed from the P8X version: -R prints a block per directory (the
    P8X indented the levels into one listing), 8 levels instead of 16,
    and the load-address column of the Y1/OS built-in dir.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/dir.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    ls, tree, find, cd, pwd, walk, num
